Cherokee towns were connected by trails  Native Americans whole lifestyle  depended on travel and when these trails  were destroyed they were cut off from  places that were sacred to him for years  and years I want to see these places  preserved I want to feel what the  Cherokees felt when they walked these  trails this great big story is inspired  by Genesis  [Music]  [Music]  my name is Lavar marshall cherokee call  me nano he D what s ki trail founder  spent the last 12 years mapping Indian  trails across the Appalachians I felt  compelled to quit my job in corporate  America and award full time to protect  the last Native American cultural sites  their history was taken away from him  and I was able to work with the Cherokee  and we bring in these records back to  the Cherokee people so that they can  teach their children and carry on their  culture and their traditions so what  we've done here is taken the oldest  historic maps in existence and use that  information and brought it forward to  the next generation of maps to early  surveys to say 1800 and then we have  reap allotted and defined exactly where  the Cherokee trails were and use this  information to produce the most modern  maps these trails represent a  significant part of the Cherokee  cultural heritage the Cherokees were  master travelers but white settlers came  in with their guns took their land and  the Cherokees were eventually expelled  all together  as I started mapping these trails I've  totaled up over a thousand mouths that  have mapped and I've walked over 200  miles of trails  [Music]  when I find work turkey trail should be  I'm looking for an impression in the  earth and I've actually come across  ancient petroglyphs carved into rock and  also some what we believe are twelve  marker trees that were bent by Cherokees  that marked certain trails lamar's  project is without a doubt one of the  most special and important pieces of  work that I've ever come across  bringing us here and showing us a real  piece of the trail where they walked is  very moving and it's so important to the  Cherokee tribes to remember this it's  important to me that I get to stand on  the place where my people were removed  at one time and I'm proud to be Church  [Music]  all the archives and all of the maps  that I produce all the records have gone  to the tribal Historic Preservation  Office so that they'll be accessible to  Cherokee people that can research their  family genealogies there's a lot more to  be discovered and we need to preserve  this so the culture of the Cherokee is  going to survive it's got to live own in  the minds and the hearts of the Cherokee  children and their youth  [Music]  [Music]
 

